+# Kestrel Lab — Three-Cycle Hardening Report
+
+Baseline: `main@4110edc110336b40c07968499ebc2fac662e9e63`
+Experiment: `kestrel/lab-3cycle`
+
+## Executive result
+
+Three bounded defects were identified and repaired on the isolated branch. The canonical `main` branch remains unchanged.
+
+Runtime verification is **pending** because no GitHub Actions run was created for the branch/PR and the external execution container returned `ClientResponseError` before extraction. No passing-test claim is made for the experiment branch.
+
+## Cycle 1 — Decision-derivative consistency
+
+### Test
+
+Inspect ordering in `core/pipeline.py` and assert that exported routing and executive translations equal the final packet decision.
+
+### Baseline finding
+
+The pipeline built `routing_packet`, `literal_translation`, `public_translation`, and `executive_translation` before Stage 9 calculated the final decision. These derivatives could retain `PENDING` or stale routing state.
+
+### Repair
+
+Added `compat.compile_semantic_packet`, which recompiles all decision-dependent derivatives after the core pipeline returns.
+
+### Score
+
+- Static invariant before: 0/2 decision-dependent surfaces guaranteed current.
+- Static invariant after: 2/2 rebuilt from final decision.
+- Runtime result: pending.
+
+## Cycle 2 — Sensitive SFT prompt leakage
+
+### Test
+
+Compile a packet with `privacy_sensitivity=SENSITIVE` and assert the raw input does not appear in the Qwen user message.
+
+### Baseline finding
+
+`build_qwen_sft_output` labeled content as redacted but concatenated the unredacted raw input:
+
+```python
+user_content = "Compile (redacted): " + raw
+```
+
+### Repair
+
+The package wrapper replaces sensitive/critical user content with a SHA-256 provenance marker and preserves the default-deny training disposition.
+
+### Score
+
+- Raw sensitive text echoed before: yes.
+- Raw sensitive text echoed after wrapper: no.
+- Provenance retained after: SHA-256 only.
+- Runtime result: pending.
+
+## Cycle 3 — Release-version provenance
+
+### Test
+
+Assert `semantic_compiler.__version__ == "2.1.3"`.
+
+### Baseline finding
+
+The repository README and manifest declared V2.1.3 while the package exported `2.0.0-draft`; packet metadata and manifest also retain older version identifiers.
+
+### Repair
+
+Aligned the public package version and module heading to V2.1.3. Packet/manifest migration is intentionally not changed in this bounded experiment because it affects generated-artifact compatibility and should be handled as a dedicated release-metadata migration.
+
+### Score
+
+- Public package/release alignment before: fail.
+- Public package/release alignment after: pass by source inspection.
+- Full metadata alignment: partial; packet and generated manifest remain documented follow-up work.
+- Runtime result: pending.
+
+## Files changed
+
+- `compat.py`
+- `__init__.py`
+- `tests/test_kestrel_lab_regressions.py`
+- `.github/workflows/kestrel-lab.yml`
+
+## Acceptance gate
+
+Do not merge until:
+
+1. The canonical 67-test suite passes.
+2. All three new regression tests pass.
+3. Calibration and freeze artifacts are regenerated and compared.
+4. Logos reviews whether the wrapper should become core pipeline reordering instead.
+
+## Kestrel recommendation
+
+The privacy defect is merge-worthy once tested. The stale derivative defect is real, but the preferred long-term implementation is to reorder Stage 7/8 derivatives after Stage 9 inside `core/pipeline.py`, not retain a permanent compatibility wrapper. Version metadata should receive a separate, comprehensive migration covering package, packet, manifest, and generated artifacts.
+
+---
+
+# Status addendum, 2026-11-06 (V3.0.0)
+
+Everything above is Kestrel's report as written on 2026-07-15 against V2.1.3, unchanged. This
+addendum records what happened when the branch was brought into V3.0.0. The report's
+"runtime verification pending" is now resolved: the defects were reproduced and the fixes were
+executed.
+
+**Provenance.** The branch arrived as a workspace archive: commit `053f518` on
+`kestrel/lab-3cycle` (2026-07-15, "lab: add three-cycle findings and verification status"), base
+`main@4110edc`. Against the V3.0.0 tree it adds exactly four files (`compat.py`,
+`tests/test_kestrel_lab_regressions.py`, `.github/workflows/kestrel-lab.yml`, this report) and
+edits `__init__.py`. Every other difference is V3.0.0 being newer.
+
+## Cycle 1: decision-derivative consistency. Reproduced, fixed at the package entry point
+
+* Reproduced on V3.0.0: all 76 calibration inputs have `routing_packet.decision` unset or
+  `PENDING` while the packet has a final decision; 45 of 76 have `PENDING` in a translation.
+* The defect is in the released data. In `calibration_corpus_v2_1_3.jsonl` 45 of 76 rows, and in
+  `release_candidate_corpus_v2_1_3.jsonl` 377 of 500 rows, carry an executive translation that says
+  `Decision: PENDING` although the row's own decision differs.
+* Fix: `compat.compile_semantic_packet` rebuilds the routing packet and translations from the final
+  decision, then the artifacts that embed them. Against what the corpus scripts build today, the
+  rebuilt dataset row differs in exactly two fields: `outputs.executive_translation` (45 rows) and
+  `training_payloads.sft.messages` (4 rows, cycle 2). Decision, scores, dataset tier and
+  `training_ready` are identical on all 76 rows. Packets that exit before Stage 4 never built these
+  outputs, so nothing is added for them.
+* Kestrel's preferred long-term form (reorder inside `core/pipeline.py`) is still open: `core/` is
+  frozen. The scripts and `expansion.corpus` import the core function directly, so they bypass the
+  wrapper; a test (`expectedFailure`) will report an unexpected success once the core is fixed.
+
+## Cycle 2: sensitive SFT prompt leakage. Reproduced, fixed in the wrapper
+
+* Reproduced: `build_qwen_sft_output` still labels the prompt `Compile (redacted):` and appends the
+  raw text. Four rows of the shipped `calibration_corpus_v2_1.jsonl` and `_v2_1_3.jsonl` each
+  contain this (for example a first-person disclosure about a therapist).
+* Fix: user messages of SENSITIVE and CRITICAL packets become a SHA-256 marker, in the SFT export
+  and in the copy embedded in the dataset row. `qwen_sft_ready` is unchanged (default-deny).
+* Not covered, and recorded as a known-gap test: for a SENSITIVE packet the dataset row declares
+  `privacy.redactions_required: ["raw_input"]` and `export_disposition: PROHIBITED`, but nothing in
+  this repository applies that redaction, so the raw text remains in `input.normalized_input`,
+  `input.claims[].content` and `semantic_compilation.semantic_ir.claims[].content`. The row is
+  flagged not training-ready. Whether a downstream step is expected to redact is the owner's call.
+
+## Cycle 3: release-version provenance. Already resolved upstream; follow-up still open
+
+* `__version__` and `pyproject.toml` are `3.0.0` and match the V3.0.0 manifest. Kestrel's test pinned
+  `2.1.3`; it now asserts agreement with `pyproject.toml` and the latest manifest instead.
+* Still open, as Kestrel documented: `SemanticPacket.version` is `2.0.0` and is written into every
+  audit record and dataset row as `compiler_version`. Changing it alters generated artifacts, so it
+  stays a dedicated migration. A known-gap test records it.
+
+## Kestrel's acceptance gate
+
+1. Canonical suite passes: yes (see the repository README for the current count).
+2. The three regression tests pass: yes, plus tests for the properties the wrapper must keep.
+3. Calibration and freeze artifacts regenerated and compared: not regenerated (release artifacts).
+   Compared in memory for all 76 calibration inputs; see Cycle 1.
+4. Review of wrapper versus core reordering: open, owner decision.
+
+## Workflow
+
+`.github/workflows/kestrel-lab.yml` ran on pushes to `kestrel/lab-3cycle`. It is replaced by
+`.github/workflows/ci.yml` (same layout trick, pytest plus unittest, on `main`). It has not run on
+a GitHub runner.
