@@ -0,0 +1,130 @@
+domain: trust_chain
+version: 1.0
+description: >-
+  Cross-cutting chain-of-trust pattern seen in Android Verified Boot, Samsung Knox secure boot,
+  DNSSEC and the RPKI: an anchor, verified delegation, freshness, an enforced failure policy,
+  a monotonic compromise record and an honest scope statement. Invariants carry source tags
+  defined in docs/DEEP_SYSTEM_INVARIANTS.md; failure modes and patterns are template-author
+  mappings.
+components:
+  - name: trust_anchor
+    function: configured root of verification that the verified system cannot rewrite
+    criticality: high
+    medical_map: immune_system
+  - name: delegation_link
+    function: one verified step that hands limited authority from a parent to a child
+    criticality: high
+    medical_map: immune_system
+  - name: verifier
+    function: checks each link before what follows is trusted or executed
+    criticality: high
+    medical_map: immune_system
+  - name: freshness_guard
+    function: rejects rolled-back, replayed or expired evidence
+    criticality: high
+    medical_map: vaccination
+  - name: failure_policy
+    function: defines and enforces what happens when verification fails
+    criticality: high
+    medical_map: quarantine
+  - name: state_record
+    function: monotonic record that compromise or deviation occurred
+    criticality: medium
+    medical_map: diagnosis
+  - name: rollover_procedure
+    function: replaces keys and objects without breaking continuous verifiability
+    criticality: medium
+    medical_map: physiotherapy
+  - name: scope_statement
+    function: states what the chain does not protect
+    criticality: medium
+    medical_map: prognosis
+  - name: channel
+    function: carries objects between parties and is assumed untrusted
+    criticality: low
+relationships:
+  - from: trust_anchor
+    to: delegation_link
+    type: authorizes_first
+  - from: delegation_link
+    to: verifier
+    type: verified_by
+  - from: freshness_guard
+    to: verifier
+    type: constrains
+  - from: verifier
+    to: failure_policy
+    type: reports_outcome_to
+  - from: failure_policy
+    to: state_record
+    type: writes
+  - from: channel
+    to: verifier
+    type: delivers_objects_to
+  - from: rollover_procedure
+    to: trust_anchor
+    type: replaces_keys_of
+  - from: scope_statement
+    to: verifier
+    type: bounds_claims_of
+invariants:
+  - "verification begins at a trust anchor the verified system cannot rewrite: a hardware root of trust in Android and Knox, a configured trust anchor in DNSSEC, and the IANA-rooted hierarchy in the RPKI [AOSP-VB][KNOX-WP][RFC4033][RFC6480]"
+  - "each link is verified by the one before it before it is trusted or run: bootloader to boot to system, DNSKEY to DS to DNSKEY to RRset, issuer certificate to subordinate certificate [AOSP-VB][RFC4033][RFC6480]"
+  - "delegation carries only the authority that was granted: a resource certificate attests allocation of specific IP and AS resources and not the identity of the holder, and a route origination authorization names one AS [RFC6480]"
+  - "authority is single-purpose where it can be: each end-entity key signs exactly one object, so revoking its certificate revokes exactly that object [RFC6480]"
+  - "old evidence is refused: rollback indexes and fuses reject older images, attestation is bound to a fresh server nonce, and expired signatures make DNSSEC data Bogus [AOSP-VB][KNOX-WP][RFC4033]"
+  - "verification outcomes are an explicit finite set rather than a boolean: Secure, Insecure, Bogus, Indeterminate [RFC4033]"
+  - "a failed verification has an enforced consequence: strict enforcement blocks boot, and a failed signature step in the secure-boot chain terminates boot [AOSP-VB][KNOX-WP]"
+  - "compromise is recorded monotonically: a permanent fuse marks that an unapproved configuration ran, and the keys it gates stay unavailable [KNOX-WP]"
+  - "the channel is untrusted: integrity comes from signatures and manifests that expose deletion or stale replacement, not from the repository or transport [RFC6480]"
+  - "the verifier sits at a higher protection level than the thing it verifies, and a monitor key generated in the normal world is a documented weakness [KW-2016][KNOX-WP]"
+  - "key and object replacement is make before break: a valid replacement exists before the old one is revoked [RFC6480]"
+  - "the chain states what it does not cover: DNSSEC gives no confidentiality or availability, the RPKI gives authorization and not identity, and Android notes that a kernel bug yielding root can bypass platform protections [RFC4033][RFC6480][AOSP-SEC]"
+failure_modes:
+  - name: anchor_compromise
+    medical_map: sepsis
+    description: the anchor is subverted or leaked, so everything verified beneath it is untrustworthy at once
+    indicators: [leaked_anchor_key, anchor_rewritten_by_subject, unverified_content_accepted_as_root]
+  - name: unverified_link
+    medical_map: chronic
+    description: a link sits outside verification coverage and persists below layers that are trusted
+    indicators: [optional_partition_unverified, unsigned_delegation, component_outside_chain]
+  - name: verifier_shares_fate_with_subject
+    medical_map: autoimmune
+    description: the verifier depends on keys or inputs generated in the domain it is meant to police
+    indicators: [monitor_key_generated_in_subject_domain, verifier_runs_at_same_privilege]
+  - name: stale_evidence_accepted
+    medical_map: pathogen
+    description: rolled-back, replayed or expired evidence is accepted and reintroduces a known weakness
+    indicators: [older_image_accepted, nonce_reused, expired_signature_ignored]
+  - name: fail_open_on_error
+    medical_map: metastasis
+    description: a failed or missing verification is treated as success and trust spreads to everything beneath it
+    indicators: [verification_error_ignored, enforcement_disabled, unverified_treated_as_verified]
+  - name: scope_misread
+    medical_map: cancer
+    description: claimed guarantees grow beyond what the chain provides, with no feedback on its stated scope, such as identity, confidentiality or availability
+    indicators: [authorization_read_as_identity, integrity_read_as_confidentiality, signed_read_as_available]
+  - name: break_before_make_rollover
+    medical_map: acute
+    description: an old key or object is withdrawn before its replacement is valid, so verification suddenly fails
+    indicators: [revoked_without_replacement, ds_dnskey_mismatch, bogus_validation_spike]
+architecture_patterns:
+  - name: anchored_chain_of_verification
+    medical_map: immune_system
+    description: every link is verified by its predecessor, back to an anchor outside the subject's control
+  - name: anti_rollback_freshness
+    medical_map: vaccination
+    description: monotonic counters, nonces and validity windows close known-bad past states
+  - name: make_before_break_rollover
+    medical_map: physiotherapy
+    description: the replacement carries load in parallel before the original is withdrawn
+  - name: single_purpose_keys
+    medical_map: quarantine
+    description: narrow keys limit the blast radius of a revocation to exactly one object
+  - name: monotonic_compromise_record
+    medical_map: diagnosis
+    description: a one-way record of deviation lets later decisions depend on what already happened
+  - name: explicit_scope_statement
+    medical_map: prognosis
+    description: published limits tell relying parties which risks remain
